### 1.1. Facts given in the task
- Review title: Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection.
- Objective: To assess the effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children 
with HIV infection, and in infants of HIV-infected mothers.
- Background: Most children with HIV live in low-income settings without ART; prevention/treatment of OIs is key. 
Cotrimoxazole is cheap, broad-spectrum, effective against PCP, safe with few side effects. Early HIV diagnosis in 
infants is complicated by maternal antibodies; prophylaxis based on maternal status is a potential solution; routine 
prophylaxis is operationally difficult and could drive resistance.
- Selection criteria: Randomised or quasi-randomised trials comparing routinely administered cotrimoxazole versus 
placebo or no treatment in:
  - Children <15 years with HIV infection; or
  - Children <18 months with HIV-infected mothers.
