One double-blind individually randomized placebo-controlled trial in Zambia (CHAP) showed that daily cotrimoxazole 
significantly reduced all-cause mortality in HIV-infected/suspected children (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002) over 
a median 19 months. Grade 3/4 adverse events were similar between arms; severe neutropenia was more frequent in 
cotrimoxazole (p=0.06).

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- Design/setting: RCT, double-blind, placebo-controlled; Zambia; DSMC recommended early stopping in Oct 2003; ITT 
analysis stated.
- Population: children 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection; 541 randomized; 7 later HIV-negative and 
excluded.
- Intervention: daily cotrimoxazole prophylaxis (dose/start not specified in abstract) vs placebo.
- Outcomes: Mortality reduced (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002); benefit consistent >12 months (HR 0.48, 95% CI 
0.27–0.84); no heterogeneity by age (p=0.82) or baseline CD4 (p=0.36). Median follow-up 19 months.
- Safety: Grade 3/4 AEs 16 (6%) vs 18 (7%); severe neutropenia 16 (6%) vs 7 (3%), p=0.06; rash reported in one placebo 
case.
- Illness episodes: PCP identified in 1/73 NPAs (placebo) among pneumonia cases; no comparative counts of pneumonia, 
SBI, or malaria reported in abstract.
- Risk of bias cues: robust blinding; ITT noted but 7 post-randomization exclusions (HIV-negative); early stopping may 
exaggerate effect; allocation concealment and attrition breakdown not detailed in abstract.

